  "subtitle": "Interrogate the computer's hidden number until it cracks",
  "why_scout_cares": "The first mass-market handheld where you play against the machine directly and the machine is the code-maker: a black-box oracle that secretly chooses a 3-5 digit number, accepts your guesses from a keypad, and answers only in coded LED verdicts. It is the consumer embodiment of Knuth's 1976 paper 'The Computer as Master Mind' — a year before Simon — and the purest example in the museum of the computer as an adversary you interrogate rather than a tool you operate. The 1979 Super-Sonic version added the delight of an audible signal when you finally strike the hidden code.",
